.Dd November 11, 2026
.Dt PKEY_ALLOC 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm pkey_alloc ,
.Nm pkey_free ,
.Nm pkey_mprotect
.Nd memory protection keys, which the processor's keys being off leave none of
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/mman.h
.Ft int
.Fn pkey_alloc "unsigned int flags" "unsigned int access_rights"
.Ft int
.Fn pkey_free "int pkey"
.Ft int
.Fn pkey_mprotect "void *addr" "size_t len" "int prot" "int pkey"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
Linux on x86-64 gives each page a protection key, whose rights a thread
sets in the PKRU register, where the processor has protection keys and
the kernel turns them on.
Rach2 does not turn them on: CR4.PKE stays clear, so the OSPKE bit of
CPUID leaf 7 reads 0 to a program, and the three calls answer as Linux's
do on a processor without keys.
.Pp
.Fn pkey_alloc
fails with
.Er EINVAL
when
.Fa flags
is not 0 or
.Fa access_rights
has a bit other than
.Dv PKEY_DISABLE_ACCESS
(1) and
.Dv PKEY_DISABLE_WRITE
(2), and otherwise with
.Er ENOSPC ,
there being no key to give.
.Pp
.Fn pkey_free
fails with
.Er EINVAL
for every key, none being allocated.
.Pp
.Fn pkey_mprotect
with
.Fa pkey
-1 is
.Xr mprotect 2 .
With any other key it checks the address and the range as
.Fn mprotect
does, returning 0 for an empty range, and then fails with
.Er EINVAL ,
the default key 0 not being one a program may name.
.Pp
Linux builds the three calls on x86-64 alone: the i686 kernel has no
entry for them and answers
.Er ENOSYS .
The x86-64 kernel answers an i386 program as it answers a 64-bit one.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn pkey_mprotect
returns 0 when
.Fa pkey
is -1 and
.Fn mprotect
succeeds, or when the range is empty.
Otherwise each call returns -1 with
.Va errno
set; the kernel returns the negative error number.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width "[ENOSPC]"
.It Bq Er EINVAL
As above; and for
.Fn pkey_mprotect ,
an address not page-aligned.
.It Bq Er ENOMEM
.Fn pkey_mprotect
with a key other than -1 was given a range that wraps.
.It Bq Er ENOSPC
.Fn pkey_alloc
has no key to give.
.It Bq Er ENOSYS
The kernel is the i686 one.
.El
.Pp
.Fn pkey_mprotect
with key -1 fails as
.Xr mprotect 2
does.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr brk 2 ,
.Xr map_shadow_stack 2 ,
.Xr mseal 2
